rst  and you may know that Yale already has  two very prestigious poetry prize as the  Bolling in prizes which is also  administered by the by Nikki library as  well as the Yale younger poets prize and  so that we wanted to make sure that when  we add poetry that we do so in a way  that enhances those prizes rather than  than taking away from them so yes  we we had 29 nominators and we asked  each nominator to make two nominations  and the two nominations were based on  two separate criteria the first was for  the author of a body of significant work  that has had a broad impact or deserves  a wider audience and the second was for  the author of work that has exhibited  outstanding promise and we asked them  specifically to make sure to nominate  under each of those categories because  it was Windham zyk split wish that we  recognize emerging writers as a part of  this prize and support them in the  practice there are in the development of  their art yes that's correct it just  happened that way we just happen to have  a great writer Adina Hoffman who  actually is with us today if any of the  press would like to talk to her if you  may want to stand up  and and that's that's really how it  happened and anybody else yes I made the  Queen we made the selections on  Wednesday that we had at the selection  committee came to town and we met all  day long and we had so thursday morning  I started calling all of the  prizewinners actually one of them jeremy  scahill i called that night because we  happen to be emailing each other at  twelve thirty in the morning and so I  made I made the call to him at about  twelve-thirty and and he said he had to  get up his mother was visiting him in  Brooklyn and he had to go wake her up to  make sure that it was real and the next  day I spent the better part of the  morning calling the prizewinners and  letting them know that they had won the  prize and I have to say it was a really  profoundly moving experience that people  were as y